    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <h2 id="how-malaysian-taxation-works" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">1. How Individual Taxation Works in Malaysia</h2>
    <p>
      Under the <strong>Malaysian Income Tax Act 1967 (Act 53)</strong>, tax is levied annually on income accruing in or derived from Malaysia. Malaysia operates a self-assessment tax regime (SST) administered by <strong>LHDNM (Hasil)</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Taxpayers fall into two main legal categories:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-sm">
      <li><strong>Tax Residents:</strong> Individuals residing physically in Malaysia for 182 days or more in a calendar year. Taxed at progressive rates from 0% to 30% and entitled to LHDN personal tax reliefs.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-Residents:</strong> Individuals residing in Malaysia for fewer than 182 days. Taxed at a flat rate of 30% without access to personal tax reliefs.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <h2 id="income-tax-brackets-2026-table" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">2. Official Malaysia Tax Rates & Brackets Table 2026</h2>
    <p>
      The table below shows the official LHDNM progressive individual tax rates for resident taxpayers in Year of Assessment 2026:
    </p>

    <!-- Tax Brackets Table -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="min-w-full border border-hairline text-left text-sm dark:border-overlay-lighter">
